.TH REGEX 3 "July 26, 2026"
.SH NAME
regcomp, regexec, regerror, regfree, re_comp, re_exec \- POSIX regular expressions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <regex.h>
.PP
.BI "int regcomp(regex_t *" preg ", const char *" pattern ", int " cflags ");"
.PP
.BI "int regexec(const regex_t *" preg ", const char *" string ,
.BI "    size_t " nmatch ", regmatch_t " pmatch[] ", int " eflags ");"
.PP
.BI "size_t regerror(int " errcode ", const regex_t *" preg ,
.BI "    char *" errbuf ", size_t " errbuf_size ");"
.PP
.BI "void regfree(regex_t *" preg ");"
.PP
.BI "char *re_comp(const char *" pattern ");"
.br
.BI "int re_exec(const char *" string ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B regcomp
compiles a POSIX basic regular expression.  With
.B REG_EXTENDED
it compiles an extended regular expression.  The implementation supports
subexpressions, alternation, back references in basic expressions, interval
quantifiers such as
.BR {2,4} ,
bracket expressions, and POSIX character classes such as
.BR [[:alpha:]] .
.PP
Compilation flags are:
.TP
.B REG_EXTENDED
Use extended rather than basic syntax.
.TP
.B REG_ICASE
Ignore case.
.TP
.B REG_NOSUB
Do not report subexpression offsets.
.TP
.B REG_NEWLINE
Make newline-sensitive matching rules apply.
.PP
.B regexec
returns zero if
.I string
matches.  If offsets are requested,
.I pmatch[0]
describes the whole match and later entries describe parenthesized
subexpressions.  Unmatched entries contain \-1.
.PP
Execution flags are:
.TP
.B REG_NOTBOL
The beginning of the supplied string is not a beginning of line.
.TP
.B REG_NOTEOL
The end of the supplied string is not an end of line.
.TP
.B REG_STARTEND
Use
.I pmatch[0]
as the input byte range, allowing embedded NUL bytes.
.PP
.B regerror
turns an error code into text and returns the buffer size required, including
the terminating NUL.
.B regfree
releases all storage owned by a compiled expression.
.PP
.B re_comp
and
.B re_exec
remain available for old BSD source.  New code should use the POSIX
interfaces because they are reentrant and allow multiple compiled patterns.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.B regexec
returns
.B REG_NOMATCH
when no match exists.  Compilation and execution errors use the
.B REG_*
codes declared in
.IR <regex.h> .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ed (1),
.BR grep (1)
